1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:10,905
In the spring of 1982, two nations went to war over a windswept cluster of islands at the bottom of the world.

2
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:15,262
Seventy-four days later, more than nine hundred people were dead.

3
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:18,504
This is the story of the Falklands War.

4
00:00:19,554 --> 00:00:25,320
The Falkland Islands lie in the South Atlantic, about three hundred miles from Argentina,

5
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:28,235
and almost eight thousand miles from Britain.

6
00:00:28,615 --> 00:00:34,568
In 1982, they were home to some eighteen hundred people, nearly all of British descent.

7
00:00:35,618 --> 00:00:38,700
Sovereignty had been disputed for two centuries.

8
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:45,634
France, Spain and Britain all planted settlements here in the 1760s, and after independence,

9
00:00:45,634 --> 00:00:49,125
Buenos Aires claimed the islands as Spain's heir.

10
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:55,301
But in January 1833, a British warship arrived, the Argentine garrison left,

11
00:00:55,301 --> 00:00:58,047
and Britain has governed ever since.

12
00:00:58,427 --> 00:01:02,555
To Argentines, they remained Las Malvinas, a lost province.

13
00:01:03,605 --> 00:01:11,614
For years, the United Nations urged both sides to negotiate, but the islanders refused any transfer of sovereignty.

14
00:01:11,994 --> 00:01:18,172
Then a 1981 British defence review announced the withdrawal of HMS Endurance,

15
00:01:18,172 --> 00:01:21,462
the Royal Navy's only ship in the region.

16
00:01:21,842 --> 00:01:25,437
In Buenos Aires, it looked like Britain no longer cared.

17
00:01:26,487 --> 00:01:32,635
Argentina was ruled by a military junta under General Leopoldo Galtieri.

18
00:01:33,015 --> 00:01:39,021
Inflation topped one hundred percent, and thousands had vanished in the regime's Dirty War.

19
00:01:39,401 --> 00:01:44,493
Recovering the Malvinas promised to unite the nation behind its generals.

20
00:01:45,543 --> 00:01:51,081
On the second of April, Argentine commandos and marines stormed ashore near Stanley.

21
00:01:51,461 --> 00:01:58,550
A small force of Royal Marines resisted for three hours, until Governor Rex Hunt ordered them to surrender.

22
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:01,465
The next day, South Georgia fell too.

23
00:02:02,515 --> 00:02:03,939
Britain was stunned.

24
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:13,092
Margaret Thatcher resolved to take the islands back, and on the fifth of April the carriers Hermes and Invincible sailed from Portsmouth.

25
00:02:13,472 --> 00:02:21,525
More than a hundred ships followed, including requisitioned liners, staging through Ascension Island on the long voyage south.

26
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:26,817
On the twenty-fifth of April, British forces retook South Georgia.

27
00:02:27,197 --> 00:02:32,362
On the first of May, a lone Vulcan bomber, refuelled again and again in mid-air,

28
00:02:32,362 --> 00:02:36,817
flew an eight-thousand-mile round trip to bomb the runway at Stanley.

29
00:02:37,867 --> 00:02:44,823
The next day, the submarine HMS Conqueror torpedoed the Argentine cruiser General Belgrano.

30
00:02:45,203 --> 00:02:50,621
Three hundred and twenty-three men died, the war's greatest single loss of life,

31
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:53,126
and still its most controversial act.

32
00:02:53,506 --> 00:02:59,927
Two days later, an Exocet missile struck the destroyer HMS Sheffield, killing twenty.

33
00:03:00,307 --> 00:03:05,138
Argentina's navy now stayed in port. The war belonged to its pilots.

34
00:03:06,188 --> 00:03:10,628
On the twenty-first of May, British troops landed at San Carlos Water.

35
00:03:11,008 --> 00:03:16,308
Argentine jets attacked at wave-top height, through what sailors called Bomb Alley.

36
00:03:16,688 --> 00:03:22,319
Ardent, Antelope, Coventry and the Atlantic Conveyor were lost, but the beachhead held.

37
00:03:23,369 --> 00:03:28,621
On the twenty-eighth, Two Para took Goose Green. Their commander, Colonel H Jones,

38
00:03:28,621 --> 00:03:31,374
was killed, and awarded the Victoria Cross.

39
00:03:31,754 --> 00:03:39,541
With most of their helicopters gone, the marines and paratroopers marched across fifty miles of bog and rock towards Stanley.

40
00:03:40,591 --> 00:03:46,572
On the eighth of June, Argentine Skyhawks caught the landing ship Sir Galahad at Fitzroy.

41
00:03:46,572 --> 00:03:49,798
Forty-eight men died, most of them Welsh Guards.

42
00:03:50,848 --> 00:03:55,465
The final battles were fought at night, in the mountains ringing Stanley.

43
00:03:55,845 --> 00:04:00,686
Mount Longdon, Two Sisters and Mount Harriet fell on the eleventh of June,

44
00:04:00,686 --> 00:04:03,956
then Tumbledown, Wireless Ridge and Mount William.

45
00:04:05,006 --> 00:04:11,567
On the fourteenth of June, General Mario Menéndez surrendered to Major General Jeremy Moore.

46
00:04:11,947 --> 00:04:14,900
After seventy-four days, the war was over.

47
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:20,707
It had cost the lives of two hundred and fifty-five British servicemen,

48
00:04:20,707 --> 00:04:24,660
six hundred and forty-nine Argentines, and three islanders.

49
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:31,055
Within days, Galtieri fell. Within eighteen months, Argentina was a democracy again.

50
00:04:31,435 --> 00:04:40,442
In 2013, the islanders voted by 99.8 percent to remain British. Argentina still claims the Malvinas.

51
00:04:40,822 --> 00:04:45,523
The dispute endures, and so does the memory of those who never came home.
